{"name":"Akaroa","hex":"#beb29a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Akaroa","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A town on Akaroa Harbour, Banks Peninsula, Canterbury, New Zealand.","A locality in Break O'Day Council, north eastern Tasmania, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Akaroa"}],"entries":[{"title":"Akaroa","blocks":[{"text":"Akaroa is a small town on Banks Peninsula in the Canterbury Region of the South Island of New Zealand, situated within a harbour of the same name. The name Akaroa is Kāi Tahu Māori for \"Long Harbour\", which would be spelled Whangaroa in standard Māori. The area was also named Port Louis-Philippe by French settlers after the reigning French king Louis Philippe I."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/AkaroaDec032024_01.jpg/330px-AkaroaDec032024_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akaroa"},{"title":"Akaroa (electorate)","blocks":[{"text":"Akaroa was a New Zealand electorate. It was located in Banks Peninsula, Canterbury, and named after the town of the same name. One of the original 24 electorates, it existed from 1853 to 1893. It was also used as an electorate for Canterbury Province."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akaroa_(electorate)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Charles_Fran%C3%A7ois_Lavaud_%281798-1878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/ea/Charles_Fran%C3%A7ois_Lavaud_%281798-1878%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Charles François Lavaud (1798–1878)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACharles_Fran%C3%A7ois_Lavaud_(1798-1878).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/AkaroaFRNames.JPG/500px-AkaroaFRNam